WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.967
Database design can be discussed at three levels.

00:00:02.967 --> 00:00:05.732
The conceptual level is the business view.

00:00:05.732 --> 00:00:12.612
We identify major subjects and high-level relationships without worrying about PostgreSQL syntax.

00:00:12.612 --> 00:00:17.237
The logical level turns that business view into a relational blueprint.

00:00:17.237 --> 00:00:24.192
We decide tables, attributes, primary keys, foreign keys, cardinality, optionality, and normalization.

00:00:24.192 --> 00:00:30.307
The physical level is the actual database implementation.

00:00:30.307 --> 00:00:35.387
Here we choose PostgreSQL data types, indexes, storage decisions, and deployment details.

00:00:35.387 --> 00:00:41.942
Keeping these levels separate is helpful because business meaning should not depend on one technical

00:00:41.942 --> 00:00:44.132
implementation.

00:00:44.132 --> 00:00:49.387
For example, the logical idea that an appointment belongs to a patient remains valid even if the physical

00:00:49.387 --> 00:00:53.652
indexing strategy changes later.

00:00:53.652 --> 00:00:59.167
Connect this idea to an online store separating customers, orders, products, and order lines.

00:00:59.167 --> 00:01:05.337
Ask what could go wrong if the concept were misunderstood: could rows be duplicated,

00:01:05.337 --> 00:01:11.987
important records disappear, categories be interpreted incorrectly, or a report become difficult to reproduce?

00:01:11.987 --> 00:01:17.062
A strong analyst can explain both the SQL operation and the assumption behind it.

00:01:17.062 --> 00:01:23.360
After the example, summarize the idea in one plain-language sentence before moving on.
